<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Low-rank orthogonalization procedures</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this part, we introduce two new low-rank orthogonalization methods as alternatives to Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.1 Low-rank orthogonalization ‣ 3 Matrix optimization with low-rank orthogonalization ‣ Low-rank orthogonalization for large-scale matrix optimization with applications to foundation model training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We first describe a low-rank orthogonalization method based on column selection. This method is based on the low-rank matrix approximation techniques developed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. For any given <math id="A1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>, it first performs column subsampling on <math id="A1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> to select <math id="A1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> columns, with <math id="A1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\ll\varrho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>ϱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\ll\varrho</annotation></semantics></math>, based on the size of each column, to construct a matrix <math id="A1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, this method performs a QR decomposition on <math id="A1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain a column-orthogonal matrix <math id="A1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}</annotation></semantics></math>, and computes <math id="A1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{msgn}(Q^{T}M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>msgn</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{msgn}(Q^{T}M)</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, it returns <math id="A1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{O}=Q\mathrm{msgn}(Q^{T}M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>O</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>msgn</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{O}=Q\mathrm{msgn}(Q^{T}M)</annotation></semantics></math> as a low-rank approximation for <math id="A1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{msgn}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>msgn</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{msgn}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>. Following the same arguments for proving (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 3.1 Low-rank orthogonalization ‣ 3 Matrix optimization with low-rank orthogonalization ‣ Low-rank orthogonalization for large-scale matrix optimization with applications to foundation model training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), <math id="A1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>O</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{O}</annotation></semantics></math> is the matrix sign of <math id="A1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QQ^{T}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QQ^{T}M</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a low-rank approximation of <math id="A1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. Details of this method are provided in Algorithm <a href="#alg5" title="Algorithm 5 ‣ Appendix A Low-rank orthogonalization procedures ‣ Low-rank orthogonalization for large-scale matrix optimization with applications to foundation model training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The following theorem, adapted from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, Theorem 4]</cite>, provides the approximation error of <math id="A1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QQ^{T}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QQ^{T}M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider Algorithm <a href="#alg5" title="Algorithm 5 ‣ Appendix A Low-rank orthogonalization procedures ‣ Low-rank orthogonalization for large-scale matrix optimization with applications to foundation model training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> with inputs <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}\cap[1,\varrho]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ϱ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}\cap[1,\varrho]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varrho:=\min\{m,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϱ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varrho:=\min\{m,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> be generated by Algorithm <a href="#alg5" title="Algorithm 5 ‣ Appendix A Low-rank orthogonalization procedures ‣ Low-rank orthogonalization for large-scale matrix optimization with applications to foundation model training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. Then, it holds that</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx51" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex49"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[\|(I-QQ^{T})M\|_{F}^{2}]\leq\|M-[M]_{r}\|_{F}^{2}+2\|M\|_{F}^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[\|(I-QQ^{T})M\|_{F}^{2}]\leq\|M-[M]_{r}\|_{F}^{2}+2\|M\|_{F}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="alg5" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 5</span> </span> A low-rank orthogonalization method based on column selection</figcaption>
<div id="alg5.3" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg5.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
            
          <span id="alg5.l1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> matrix <math id="alg5.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{M}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{M}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>, rank trial <math id="alg5.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}\cap[1,\varrho]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ϱ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}\cap[1,\varrho]</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg5.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
            
          <span id="alg5.l2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output:</span> approximate matrix sign <math id="alg5.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{O}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>O</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{O}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg5.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
            
          Compute sampling probabilities <math id="alg5.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{p_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{p_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="alg5.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}=\|M_{(i)}\|^{2}/\|M\|_{F}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}=\|M_{(i)}\|^{2}/\|M\|_{F}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="alg5.l3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,n</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg5.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
            
          <span id="alg5.l4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg5.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1,2,\ldots,r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1,2,\ldots,r</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg5.l4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg5.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            Choose <math id="alg5.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{t}\in\{1,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{t}\in\{1,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> with probability <math id="alg5.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(i_{t}=i)=p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(i_{t}=i)=p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="alg5.l5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,n</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg5.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            Set <math id="alg5.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{(i)}=M_{(i_{t})}/\sqrt{rp_{i_{t}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{(i)}=M_{(i_{t})}/\sqrt{rp_{i_{t}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg5.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
            
          <span id="alg5.l7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg5.l7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg5.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
            
          Perform a QR decomposition on <math id="alg5.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> to get a column-orthogonal Q factor <math id="alg5.l8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg5.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
            
          Return <math id="alg5.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{O}=Q\mathrm{msgn}(Q^{T}M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>O</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>msgn</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{O}=Q\mathrm{msgn}(Q^{T}M)</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="A1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We next propose a low-rank orthogonalization method based on power iterations. This method is based on the low-rank matrix approximation techniques developed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>. Similar to Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.1 Low-rank orthogonalization ‣ 3 Matrix optimization with low-rank orthogonalization ‣ Low-rank orthogonalization for large-scale matrix optimization with applications to foundation model training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, this method also uses a Gaussian random matrix <math id="A1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\ll\varrho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>ϱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\ll\varrho</annotation></semantics></math>, and performs a QR decomposition on <math id="A1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(MM^{T})^{q}MG" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(MM^{T})^{q}MG</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="A1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain a column-orthogonal matrix <math id="A1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}</annotation></semantics></math>. The method then computes <math id="A1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{msgn}(Q^{T}M)\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>msgn</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{msgn}(Q^{T}M)\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> and returns <math id="A1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{O}=Q\mathrm{msgn}(Q^{T}M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>O</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>msgn</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{O}=Q\mathrm{msgn}(Q^{T}M)</annotation></semantics></math> as a low-rank approximation of <math id="A1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{msgn}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>msgn</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{msgn}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>. Following the same arguments used to prove (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 3.1 Low-rank orthogonalization ‣ 3 Matrix optimization with low-rank orthogonalization ‣ Low-rank orthogonalization for large-scale matrix optimization with applications to foundation model training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), <math id="A1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>O</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{O}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the matrix sign of <math id="A1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QQ^{T}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QQ^{T}M</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a low-rank approximation of <math id="A1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. Details of this method are provided in Algorithm <a href="#alg6" title="Algorithm 6 ‣ Appendix A Low-rank orthogonalization procedures ‣ Low-rank orthogonalization for large-scale matrix optimization with applications to foundation model training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, and it can be seen that the method reduces to Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.1 Low-rank orthogonalization ‣ 3 Matrix optimization with low-rank orthogonalization ‣ Low-rank orthogonalization for large-scale matrix optimization with applications to foundation model training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> when <math id="A1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg6" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 6</span> </span> A low-rank orthogonalization procedure based on power iterations</figcaption>
<div id="alg6.3" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg6.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
            
          <span id="alg6.l1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> matrix <math id="alg6.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{M}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{M}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>, rank trial <math id="alg6.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}\cap[1,\varrho]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ϱ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}\cap[1,\varrho]</annotation></semantics></math>, exponent <math id="alg6.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg6.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
            
          <span id="alg6.l2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output:</span> approximate matrix sign <math id="alg6.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{O}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>O</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{O}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg6.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
            
          Generate a Gaussian random matrix <math id="alg6.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg6.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
            
          Perform a QR decomposition on <math id="alg6.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(MM^{T})^{q}MG" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(MM^{T})^{q}MG</annotation></semantics></math> to get a column-orthogonal Q factor <math id="alg6.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg6.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
            
          Return <math id="alg6.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{O}=Q\mathrm{msgn}(Q^{T}M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>O</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>msgn</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{O}=Q\mathrm{msgn}(Q^{T}M)</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="A1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The following theorem, adapted from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, Section 8.2]</cite>, provides the approximation error of <math id="A1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QQ^{T}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QQ^{T}M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider Algorithm <a href="#alg6" title="Algorithm 6 ‣ Appendix A Low-rank orthogonalization procedures ‣ Low-rank orthogonalization for large-scale matrix optimization with applications to foundation model training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> with inputs <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}\cap[1,\varrho]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ϱ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}\cap[1,\varrho]</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varrho:=\min\{m,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϱ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varrho:=\min\{m,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> be generated by Algorithm <a href="#alg5" title="Algorithm 5 ‣ Appendix A Low-rank orthogonalization procedures ‣ Low-rank orthogonalization for large-scale matrix optimization with applications to foundation model training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. Then, for any <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{*}\leq r-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{*}\leq r-2</annotation></semantics></math>, it holds that</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx52" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex50"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[\|(I-QQ^{T})M\|^{2}]\leq\bigg(1+\sqrt{\frac{r_{*}}{r-r_{*}-1}}+e\sqrt{\frac{r}{r-r_{*}}}\cdot\sqrt{\varrho-r_{*}}\bigg)^{1/(2q+1)}\sigma_{r_{*}+1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>ϱ</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[\|(I-QQ^{T})M\|^{2}]\leq\bigg(1+\sqrt{\frac{r_{*}}{r-r_{*}-1}}+e\sqrt{\frac{r}{r-r_{*}}}\cdot\sqrt{\varrho-r_{*}}\bigg)^{1/(2q+1)}\sigma_{r_{*}+1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{r_{*}+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{r_{*}+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r_{*}+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r_{*}+1)</annotation></semantics></math>th largest singular value of <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Experimental details</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this part, we present the details for our experiments on pretraining GPT-2 and LLaMA models.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A2.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Appendix B Experimental details ‣ Low-rank orthogonalization for large-scale matrix optimization with applications to foundation model training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> presents the hyperparameter configurations for the GPT-2 and LLaMA models. We employ hyperparameters aligned with those used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite>. In addition, we set the maximum training sequence length to 65,536 tokens, the maximum validation sequence length to 262,144 tokens, and the validation dataset size to 10,485,760 tokens.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Hyperparameter configurations for GPT-2 and LLaMA models.</figcaption>
<table id="A2.T3.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A2.T3.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T3.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><math id="A2.T3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>#</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.T3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> Parameters</span></th>
<td id="A2.T3.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A2.T3.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hidden Dimension</span></td>
<td id="A2.T3.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A2.T3.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Intermediate Dimension</span></td>
<td id="A2.T3.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><math id="A2.T3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>#</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.T3.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> Heads</span></td>
<td id="A2.T3.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><math id="A2.T3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>#</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.T3.1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> Layers</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T3.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T3.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">60M</th>
<td id="A2.T3.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">512</td>
<td id="A2.T3.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">1376</td>
<td id="A2.T3.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">8</td>
<td id="A2.T3.1.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">8</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T3.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T3.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row">130M</th>
<td id="A2.T3.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">768</td>
<td id="A2.T3.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">2048</td>
<td id="A2.T3.1.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">12</td>
<td id="A2.T3.1.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">12</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T3.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T3.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row">350M</th>
<td id="A2.T3.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">1024</td>
<td id="A2.T3.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">2736</td>
<td id="A2.T3.1.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">16</td>
<td id="A2.T3.1.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">24</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T3.1.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T3.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">1B</th>
<td id="A2.T3.1.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">2048</td>
<td id="A2.T3.1.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">5461</td>
<td id="A2.T3.1.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">24</td>
<td id="A2.T3.1.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">32</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We present the algorithmic hyperparameter search grids for each method in Table <a href="#A2.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Appendix B Experimental details ‣ Low-rank orthogonalization for large-scale matrix optimization with applications to foundation model training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, following a similar choice as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>. The best numerical performance for each training method across the grids is presented in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Numerical experiments ‣ Low-rank orthogonalization for large-scale matrix optimization with applications to foundation model training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. In addition, we apply a warm-up schedule for the learning rate during the first 70% of the training iterations, followed by a cosine annealing schedule that reduces the learning rate to 10% of its initial value.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>Algorithmic hyperparameters. Here, ‘NS’ stands for Newton-Schulz.</figcaption>
<table id="A2.T4.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A2.T4.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T4.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="A2.T4.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method</span></td>
<td id="A2.T4.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="A2.T4.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hyperparameters</span></td>
<td id="A2.T4.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="A2.T4.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Values</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T4.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T4.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" rowspan="3"><span id="A2.T4.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text">SGDM</span></td>
<td id="A2.T4.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">batch size</td>
<td id="A2.T4.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">2</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T4.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T4.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">learning rate</td>
<td id="A2.T4.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">{1e-4, 1e-3, 1e-2}</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T4.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T4.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">weight decay</td>
<td id="A2.T4.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">{0, 1e-3, 1e-2}</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T4.1.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T4.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" rowspan="3"><span id="A2.T4.1.5.1.1" class="ltx_text">AdamW</span></td>
<td id="A2.T4.1.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">batch size</td>
<td id="A2.T4.1.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">2</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T4.1.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T4.1.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">learning rate</td>
<td id="A2.T4.1.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">{1e-4, 1e-3, 1e-2}</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T4.1.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T4.1.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">weight decay</td>
<td id="A2.T4.1.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">{0, 1e-3, 1e-2}</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T4.1.8" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T4.1.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" rowspan="4"><span id="A2.T4.1.8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Muon</span></td>
<td id="A2.T4.1.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">batch size</td>
<td id="A2.T4.1.8.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">2</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T4.1.9" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T4.1.9.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">learning rate</td>
<td id="A2.T4.1.9.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">{1e-2, 2.5e-2, 5e-2, 1e-3}</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T4.1.10" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T4.1.10.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="A2.T4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>#</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#</annotation></semantics></math> NS steps</td>
<td id="A2.T4.1.10.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">{5, 10, 20}</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T4.1.11" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T4.1.11.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">weight decay</td>
<td id="A2.T4.1.11.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">0</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T4.1.12" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T4.1.12.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" rowspan="5"><span id="A2.T4.1.12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Low rank Muon</span></td>
<td id="A2.T4.1.12.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">batch size</td>
<td id="A2.T4.1.12.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">2</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T4.1.13" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T4.1.13.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">learning rate</td>
<td id="A2.T4.1.13.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">{1e-2, 2.5e-2, 5e-2, 1e-3}</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T4.1.14" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T4.1.14.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">rank</td>
